# ==============================================================================
# AquariusOS — the creator apps that come with the operating system
# ==============================================================================
# PLAIN ENGLISH
#
# This file is a SHOPPING LIST. It does not install anything by itself, and
# nothing on it is installed without being asked for. It says "these are the
# apps AquariusOS suggests" — and at the first login a window shows them, with
# the everyday ones already ticked, and installs the ones left ticked.
#
# ⚠️ CHANGED 2026-09-04. Until then this list WAS installed automatically: a
#    service fetched every entry on the first boot the machine was online,
#    about ten gigabytes, whether or not anybody wanted any of it. Downloading
#    that much of other people's software before its owner has said yes is not
#    a decision an operating system should make, so now it asks. The apps have
#    not changed and neither has this file — what changed is who presses go.
#
# Every app on this list is a Flatpak — a self-contained app that comes from
# Flathub, the Linux app store, and updates on its author's schedule instead of
# ours. That is standing decision 4 of this project: creator apps ship as
# Flatpaks, offered rather than baked into the operating system. It is why the
# installer stays about four gigabytes instead of about fifteen, and it is why
# a Blender release from next Tuesday reaches you next Tuesday.
#
# THE PRICE, SAID OUT LOUD: the apps are NOT inside the installer. Whatever is
# ticked has to be downloaded, which on a normal home connection is roughly ten
# to twenty minutes if everything is kept, and less if it is not. You can use
# the computer the whole time. If the machine is not online yet, nothing breaks
# and nothing is lost — the same window, or `aq apps install`, will do the job
# whenever it is.
#
# ⚠️ THIS FILE IS FLATPAK'S FORMAT, AND ONLY FLATPAK'S KEYS BELONG IN IT.
#    The names, descriptions and categories the chooser window shows live in
#    /usr/share/aquarius/apps/catalog.ini, one entry per ID below. The two must
#    describe exactly the same set of apps, and the build fails if they do not.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FILE FORMAT — Flatpak's own, not something we invented
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `flatpak preinstall` is Flatpak's built-in support for exactly this job and
# `.preinstall` files are its own format (`man flatpak-preinstall`). Flatpak
# reads two folders:
#
#     /usr/share/flatpak/preinstall.d/   ours — this file
#     /etc/flatpak/preinstall.d/         yours — overrides ours, per machine
#
# Each block's heading is an app's full Flathub ID. The keys are:
#
#     Install=true|false   whether to install it. Missing means true.
#     Branch=stable        WHICH VERSION LINE. NOT OPTIONAL — see below.
#     IsRuntime=true       set on plug-ins and runtime extensions, which are
#                          not applications and are not found without it.
#
# ⚠️ Branch= IS NOT OPTIONAL. Leave it out and Flatpak looks for a branch
#    called "master", which Flathub apps do not have, and the install quietly
#    finds nothing at all. Every block below says Branch=stable except the one
#    that genuinely lives on a different branch, which is commented where it is.
#
# ⚠️ A TYPO IN AN ID IS SILENT. Nothing appears, nothing complains. So the
#    build asks Flathub, at build time, whether every ID below really exists
#    (build_files/64-creator-apps.sh, "Ask Flathub whether these apps are real")
#    and refuses to publish an image with a made-up name in this file.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW A PERSON REMOVES ONE OF THESE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Open Software (the app store), find the app, press Uninstall. Or in a
# terminal:
#
#     flatpak uninstall com.obsproject.Studio
#
# AND IT STAYS GONE. Flatpak remembers that you removed a preinstalled app and
# never puts it back — that is a guarantee of `flatpak preinstall` itself, not
# something we bolted on. The same is true of every app here. AquariusOS
# suggests; it does not insist.
#
# To stop one being installed in the first place — on a machine you are setting
# up for somebody else, say — write the opposite into your own copy:
#
#     sudo mkdir -p /etc/flatpak/preinstall.d
#     printf '[Flatpak Preinstall org.blender.Blender]\nInstall=false\n' \
#       | sudo tee /etc/flatpak/preinstall.d/00-local.preinstall
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TO ADD AN APP
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy a block, change the ID, keep Branch=stable. Check the ID really exists
# first: https://flathub.org/apps/<the-id> should load. Beware — that page
# answers "yes" to almost anything, because it is drawn in your browser; the
# honest test, and the one the build uses, is
# https://flathub.org/api/v2/appstream/<the-id> which returns nothing at all
# for a name that does not exist.
#
# The beginner write-up: docs/restart/creator-apps.md
# ==============================================================================


# ==============================================================================
# VIDEO
# ==============================================================================

# OBS Studio — recording and live streaming. The one app on this list that
# needs help from the operating system as well as from Flathub:
#
#   * the virtual camera (the button that makes OBS appear as a webcam in
#     Zoom, Meet or a browser) needs a kernel module called v4l2loopback, which
#     a Flatpak cannot install. AquariusOS ships it — build_files/62-virtual-
#     camera.sh — and loads it at boot.
#   * cameras and capture cards need permission that Flathub's default sandbox
#     does not grant. AquariusOS grants it —
#     /usr/share/aquarius/flatpak-overrides/com.obsproject.Studio.
#
# Screen capture needs neither: it goes through the desktop's screen-sharing
# portal, which is part of the session floor (build_files/30-session.sh) and
# was proved working on the bench on 2026-09-03.
[Flatpak Preinstall com.obsproject.Studio]
Branch=stable

# Kdenlive — the free video editor. It is here as the everyday cutter that
# opens anything: unlike the free DaVinci Resolve it reads H.264, H.265 and AAC
# straight off a camera with no conversion at all. When Resolve refuses a file
# and you do not want to wait for a conversion, this is the way in.
[Flatpak Preinstall org.kde.kdenlive]
Branch=stable


# ==============================================================================
# THE OBS PLUG-INS
# ==============================================================================
# A plug-in is not an application, so each of these needs IsRuntime=true. Get
# that wrong and Flatpak looks for an app by that name, finds none, and says
# nothing.
#
# These four are the ones a creator on Linux actually misses, and they are
# nearly the same set that Bazzite — the gaming distribution with the largest
# streaming audience on Linux — preinstalls for exactly this reason.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OBS VkCapture — capture a game's own picture directly (Vulkan and OpenGL),
# instead of capturing the screen and everything on top of it.
[Flatpak Preinstall com.obsproject.Studio.Plugin.OBSVkCapture]
Branch=stable
IsRuntime=true

# The other half of VkCapture, and THE PART EVERYONE FORGETS. This is a Vulkan
# layer: the piece that sits inside the GAME and hands its frames over. Install
# the plug-in without this and game capture silently records nothing.
#
# ⚠️ ITS BRANCH IS NOT "stable". A Vulkan layer belongs to a version of the
#    Freedesktop runtime, and its branch is that version number. 25.08 is what
#    Flathub publishes today and what Bazzite pins. When the Freedesktop
#    runtime moves on, this line has to move with it — the build's Flathub
#    check will tell you the ID is fine while the install quietly finds no such
#    branch, so it is written down here as the thing to look at first if game
#    capture stops arriving.
[Flatpak Preinstall org.freedesktop.Platform.VulkanLayer.OBSVkCapture]
Branch=25.08
IsRuntime=true

# PipeWire Audio Capture — capture ONE application's sound (the game, the
# browser tab, the call) instead of everything the speakers are playing. On a
# modern Linux desktop this is the plug-in that makes a stream mixable, and OBS
# does not ship it built in.
[Flatpak Preinstall com.obsproject.Studio.Plugin.PipeWireAudioCapture]
Branch=stable
IsRuntime=true

# GStreamer — lets OBS record and stream through GStreamer's encoders as well
# as its own.
[Flatpak Preinstall com.obsproject.Studio.Plugin.Gstreamer]
Branch=stable
IsRuntime=true

# GStreamer VA-API — hardware video encoding on AMD and Intel graphics, so
# recording does not cost the processor. (On NVIDIA, OBS uses NVENC, which is
# built in and needs no plug-in.)
#
# Note the capital S in "GStreamerVaapi" and the small s in "Gstreamer" above.
# That is not a typo in this file: the two plug-ins really are spelled
# differently on Flathub.
[Flatpak Preinstall com.obsproject.Studio.Plugin.GStreamerVaapi]
Branch=stable
IsRuntime=true


# ==============================================================================
# AUDIO
# ==============================================================================

# Audacity — the quick one. Record a voiceover, top and tail a file, clean up
# a hiss. What most video work actually needs from an audio program.
[Flatpak Preinstall org.audacityteam.Audacity]
Branch=stable

# Ardour — the full multitrack studio, for when Audacity is not enough: a music
# bed, a podcast with several microphones, a real mix.
[Flatpak Preinstall org.ardour.Ardour]
Branch=stable


# ==============================================================================
# PICTURES AND 3D
# ==============================================================================

# Krita — digital painting and illustration. Also the best free tool on Linux
# for a hand-drawn thumbnail.
[Flatpak Preinstall org.kde.krita]
Branch=stable

# GIMP — photo and image editing. The Photoshop-shaped hole.
[Flatpak Preinstall org.gimp.GIMP]
Branch=stable

# Inkscape — vector drawing. Logos, titles, anything that has to stay sharp at
# any size.
[Flatpak Preinstall org.inkscape.Inkscape]
Branch=stable

# Blender — 3D, motion graphics and, quietly, one of the better video editors
# on Linux. The biggest download on this list by a distance.
[Flatpak Preinstall org.blender.Blender]
Branch=stable


# ==============================================================================
# THE DESK
# ==============================================================================

# Obsidian — notes and writing in plain Markdown files. Listed here alongside
# Aquarius Writer, which is built into the OS, because they are not the same
# job: Writer is a studio for long-form work, Obsidian is where the rest of a
# working life is written down.
[Flatpak Preinstall md.obsidian.Obsidian]
Branch=stable

# LocalSend — send a file from your phone to this computer, and back, over your
# own network. No cloud, no account, no cable. For a creator this is how
# footage shot on a phone gets onto the machine in ten seconds.
[Flatpak Preinstall org.localsend.localsend_app]
Branch=stable

# Google Chrome. Firefox is NOT on this list, and that is deliberate: Firefox
# is installed as an ordinary system package (build_files/40-gnome-desktop.sh),
# so it is in the image and works the moment the machine boots, with no
# download at all. Chrome is here because plenty of creator tooling — upload
# dashboards, Meet, some editors' web consoles — is written against it.
#
# ⚠️ Chrome is an odd one and it fails differently from everything else here.
#    Flathub is not allowed to hand out Chrome itself, so the Flatpak is a
#    small wrapper that downloads Google's own installer at install time. When
#    Google removes an old build before Flathub catches up, this one app fails
#    while everything else installs perfectly. The service handles exactly that:
#    it installs what it can and tries the stragglers again at the next start.
[Flatpak Preinstall com.google.Chrome]
Branch=stable


# ==============================================================================
# GAMING
# ==============================================================================
# ⚠️ STEAM IS NOT ON THIS LIST AND MUST NOT BE ADDED TO IT.
#
# Steam is built into AquariusOS as an ordinary system program, installed by
# build_files/68-gaming.sh from the Terra repository, exactly as Bazzite does
# it. That is not an inconsistency with "creator apps ship as Flatpaks" — it is
# the one place where the sandbox is the problem rather than the solution. A
# sandboxed Steam cannot see controllers properly, cannot reach an external
# drive full of games without being told to, and keeps its own separate copy of
# the graphics driver that has to match the system's. Every gaming-focused
# Linux ships Steam as a system package for those three reasons.
#
# So Steam is already there when the machine starts, and the chooser shows it
# under "Included with AquariusOS" rather than as something to install.
#
# The three below are the OTHER game launchers — the ones worth offering and
# not worth assuming. None of them is ticked by default
# (Recommended=false in catalog.ini).
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Heroic — the launcher for games bought on Epic, GOG and Amazon. Steam is not
# the only shop, and none of the others has a Linux client of their own.
[Flatpak Preinstall com.heroicgameslauncher.hgl]
Branch=stable

# Lutris — the launcher for everything that is not on a modern shop at all: old
# Windows discs, itch.io downloads, emulators, Battle.net. It runs Windows games
# through umu-launcher, which AquariusOS installs as a system package, so it
# works properly here rather than downloading its own copy.
[Flatpak Preinstall net.lutris.Lutris]
Branch=stable

# ProtonUp-Qt — installs alternative versions of Proton (GE-Proton and friends).
# Occasionally a Windows game will not run on the Proton that comes with Steam
# and runs perfectly on a community build. This is the button for that, and it
# saves a person from following a forum post full of terminal commands.
[Flatpak Preinstall net.davidotek.pupgui2]
Branch=stable
